      "Question": "The measured spectrum strongly contains an external-light component that appears unrelated to the sample. Check the cause and recover the measurement quality.",
      "Ans": "To identify the cause of the external-light component and recover the measurement quality, I will perform a comparative analysis between the current instrument state and the suspected problematic data (`ps_strong_bg.csv`).\n\n### 1. Analysis of Symptom: \"External Light\"\nI have observed a highly unusual behavior during my initial diagnostic measurements:\n*   **Shutter Open (Laser 40%):** Max intensity $\\approx 12,328$ - $18,820$.\n*   **Shutter Closed (Laser 40%):** Max intensity $\\approx 16,347$.\n*   **Shutter Open (Laser $\\approx 0\\%$):** Max intensity $\\approx 662$.\n\nIn a healthy system, closing the shutter should block both the laser and ambient light, resulting in a \"dark frame\" with minimal intensity. The fact that **closing the shutter increases the signal** (from $\\sim 12\\text{k}$ to $\\sim 16\\text{k}$) and introduces over **300 structured peaks** suggests that this is not simple room-light leakage, but rather an instrument anomaly—likely electronic interference or a hardware-induced artifact triggered by the shutter mechanism.\n\n### 2. Verification of Provided Data\nI attempted to analyze `ps_strong_bg.csv` from your attached files. Based on the filename and your request, this file likely contains a spectrum plagued by the same \"external light\" issue. By comparing its spectral profile (peak positions and baseline) with the \"shutter-closed\" artifact I just measured, I can confirm if the cause is systemic to the hardware.\n\n### 3. Recovery Plan\nTo recover measurement quality, I will follow these steps:\n1.  **Baseline Identification:** Measure a true dark frame (Shutter Closed) and an ambient light frame (Laser OFF/Minimum, Shutter Open) to characterize the noise floor.\n2.  **Source Isolation:** If the \"external light\" is indeed structured room lighting (e.g., fluorescent lamps), I will recommend checking the light-tightness of the sample chamber or utilizing a narrower bandpass filter if available.\n3.  **Background Subtraction:** For existing data, I will apply an Iterative Polynomial Background Subtraction (IPBSA) to remove the external component, provided it is a smooth baseline. If it consists of sharp \"external\" peaks, these must be identified and subtracted using a reference background spectrum measured at a blank location on the substrate.\n\n**Next Action:**\nI will now run a final analysis to compare the `ps_strong_bg.csv` data against my diagnostic \"shutter-closed\" spectrum to confirm the exact nature of the interference.",
